            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedJson": {
              "dependsOn": "randomness"
            },
            "virtualMs": 0,
            "wholeRunCantCheckOk": "randomness",
            "returnedType": "array"
          },
          "why": "list() of a set of strings: its order depends on Python's hash seed, random per process. Honest: not a plain fact (or run under a fixed seed and say so)."
        },
        {
          "id": "q01b",
          "exportName": "int_keys",
          "args": [],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedNotJson": {
              "type": "object",
              "reasonMentions": "key"
            },
            "virtualMs": 0
          },
          "why": "{1: 'a', True: 'b', '1': 'c'} is {1: 'b', '1': 'c'} in Python (1 and True are the same key); a non-text key is not JSON. Its type is object."
        },
        {
          "id": "q01c",
          "exportName": "tuple_inside",
          "args": [],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedNotJson": {
              "type": "array",
              "reasonMentions": "tuple"
            },
            "virtualMs": 0
          },
          "why": "A list holding a tuple. The returned value is a list (type array); it is not JSON because of the tuple inside. Atlas currently reports the inner item's type (review B-1)."
        },
        {
          "id": "q01d",
          "exportName": "float_two",
          "args": [],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedJson": 2,
            "returnedType": "number",
            "virtualMs": 0
          },
          "why": "2.0 is written 2 under Atlas's stated JSON rule (JavaScript number forms); a convention, stated in the pass rule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q02",
      "file": "cases/p02-timing.py",
      "language": "py",
      "category": "py timing",
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "q02a",
          "exportName": "block_then_await",
          "args": [
            {
              "kind": "stand-in",
              "name": "fetch",
              "async": true,
              "then": "repeat_last",
              "calls": [
                {
                  "do": "return",
                  "value": "v",
                  "afterMs": 100
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedJson": [
              "v",
              500
            ],
            "returnedType": "array",
            "virtualMs": 500,
            "calls": {
              "fetch": 1
            }
          },
          "why": "The task's fetch answers at 100 ms, but time.sleep(0.5) blocks the loop until 500 ms; then the task is already done. Returns ['v', 500] at 500 ms. Real Python agrees."
        },
        {
          "id": "q02b",
          "exportName": "deadline",
          "args": [
            {
              "kind": "stand-in",
              "name": "fetch",
              "async": true,
              "then": "repeat_last",
              "calls": [
                {
                  "do": "return",
                  "value": "v",
                  "afterMs": 500
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedJson": "timed out",
            "returnedType": "string",
            "virtualMs": 100,
            "calls": {
              "fetch": 1
            }
          },
          "why": "wait_for with 0.1 s against a 0.5 s answer: TimeoutError at 100 ms, caught. Real Python agrees."
        },
        {
          "id": "q02c",
          "exportName": "cpu_elapsed",
          "args": [],
          "expect": {
            "outcome": "returned",
            "returnedJson": {
              "dependsOn": "clock"
            },
            "virtualMs": 0,
            "wholeRunCantCheckOk": "clock",
            "returnedType": "boolean"
          },
          "why": "perf_counter around CPU work: a real run takes more than 1 ms; Atlas's clock does not move during CPU work. Honest: not a plain fact."
        }
      ]
    },
